      "dom_id": "question-133429",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{5}$ is irrational\n\nII. $\\sqrt{5}+a$ is always irrational\n\nIII. $a+\\sqrt{5}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $a=-\\sqrt{5},$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}+a=\\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{5}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is rational.\n\n- Statement III is true. From part II, we know that $\\sqrt{5}+a$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational. Since addition is commutative, we conclude that $a+\\sqrt{5}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
